# Roots — store slots 4, 5, 6: the boss fight, the killing blow, the victory.
#
# THE REGULAR FIGHT, NEVER THE BOSSRUN. The old *_seven_slots.keys reached the
# boss with KEY 8 from the session menu — the Tier Skip Challenge, which runs
# sandbox_mode: its battle order is card_difficulty.split_cluster, two buckets
# shuffled, NOT the interval sort. Every boss frame shot that way was a gauntlet
# fight (found on The Guide, 2026-09-16). The path below is Know Your Enemy:
#   5  session menu -> Enter the Sanctum      (the menu takes 5-8 only; 4 is
#                                              ignored here and does nothing)
#   4  sanctum -> KNOW YOUR ENEMY
#   1  the cluster
#   5  the confirm screen -> fight
# Readiness comes from the forge: run with FLASHBOSS_KIT_FIGHT_READY=1, which
# stamps kit card 1 at interval 34 and card 8 at 13, so battle_order[0] is card 1
# and the fight opens on it. Without the stamp the cluster reads 0/8 and the
# confirm screen offers no fight at all.
#
# INVERTED BOSS: the definition is the question, the eight options are the cards'
# own TargetWords. ANSWER reads the live grid and presses the word the definition
# describes; the map is {TargetWord: [definition]} from the kit.
LOG Roots — store route D

{LANG_KEYS}
KEY ENTER
EXPECT Core
KEY 1

# past lesson 000 and GETTING STARTED, then out of the review
KEY 5
KEY 5
KEY 5
KEY ENTER
KEY ENTER
KEY ENTER
KEY 9
KEY 9
EXPECT Core
KEY 1

# ── Know Your Enemy -> the cluster -> fight ──────────────────────────────
KEY 5
KEY 4
KEY 1
KEY 5
# NO key at the "Engaging Boss" splash: a timed hold that reads nothing.

# ── slot 4: the opening frame, hero on rung 1 ────────────────────────────
# Position 1 prints the danger-zone line (boss_fight: the initial status at
# position 1), which is the true first frame of a fresh fight.

# ── FILM: every beat of the fight, for the site GIF ──────────────────────
# The store route takes three stills. A GIF wants the beats between them, and in
# particular it wants the ANSWER FREEZE and the REPOSITION as one frame, because
# the game moves them together: answering redraws the question AND repositions
# the hero and the boss AND changes the status line under the platform, all on
# the same beat. (Odiin, 2026-09-20, on the web demo getting this wrong: "the
# text changes on the answer, not on the hero/boss reposition like it should.")
#
# DO NOT PUT A KEY BETWEEN TWO ANSWERS. This route used to send KEY ENTER after
# each answer freeze, on the assumption that the fight waits to be advanced. It
# does not — ANSWER moves it on by itself. ENTER is outside the 1-8/9 the prompt
# accepts, so the game printed "Invalid input. Choose 1-8 or 9 to retreat:" and
# THAT LINE SCROLLED THE TERMINAL: the header box lost its top edge and the whole
# screen sat two lines high for the shot that followed. Three such frames reached
# the shipped GIFs before Odiin caught them on 2026-09-20 — "your invalid input
# shots are in the gif … every other shot is not needed. it breaks the frame."
# The only key this fight accepts between answers is a digit, and it does not
# need one.
EXPECT [1-8]\.
EXPECT ₃    ₄
REFUTE ₁    ₂
EXPECT ╚═+╝\s*\Z
SHOT frame01
ANSWER
SHOT frame02
ANSWER
EXPECT [1-8]\.
EXPECT ₁    ₂
REFUTE ₃    ₄
SHOT frame03
ANSWER
SHOT frame04
ANSWER
SHOT frame05
# ENTER *is* valid here and only here: the victory screen says "Enter to continue".
KEY ENTER
# ── the cluster victory: the real game's graduation screen ───────────────
EXPECT {CARD1}
EXPECT 🎆\s*\Z
SHOT frame06
# '8' toggles the word list between the pack language and English, in place.
# ONE toggle only: a second 8 returns to the first list and the shot is a
# pixel-identical duplicate, which clean_frames.sh would drop anyway.
KEY 8
SHOT frame07
LOG film route taken
END
